 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a string containing JSON, with syntax highlighting. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON, so it is valid JSON. The ``indent`` argument sets the number of spaces to indent (default 4), and ``highlight`` can be set to ``False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Logging a JSON renderable works in the same way, using :class:`rich.json.JSON`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "rich"}'))
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, so its lines are not wrapped when printed or logged. Under default printing, lines wider than the console are cropped to the console width. Enable soft wrapping (for example ``console.print(JSON(data), soft_wrap=True)``) to keep long lines whole.
+
+The package also provides a :func:`rich.print_json` function, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+From the command line, you can pretty print a JSON file with the ``rich.json`` module. The file path is required::
+
+    python -m rich.json FILE
+
